# Claude Code Reusable Agent Prompts

This repository contains a reusable three-agent workflow for Claude Code. I made it so I can start a project with separate agents responsible for building, verifying, and fixing the work instead of asking one agent to handle everything in the same context.

The prompts are designed to work across different projects, languages, and operating systems. The project requirements change, but the agent roles and review process stay consistent.

## Agent Team

| Agent | Responsibility | Can edit project code? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `project-builder` | Reads the requirements and implements the project | Yes |
| `project-verifier` | Independently tests the completed work and documents findings | No |
| `bug-fixer` | Reproduces and repairs confirmed verifier findings | Yes |

This creates a simple workflow:

```text
Requirements
    ↓
Project Builder
    ↓
Project Verifier
    ↓
Bug Fixer, if needed
    ↓
Final Verification
```

## Why I Made This

I wanted a repeatable way to use Claude Code agents without rebuilding the workflow for every project. Separating the roles gives the verifier a fresh context and keeps it from quietly changing the code it is supposed to review.

The bug fixer also works from documented findings instead of making random changes or adding unrelated features.

## Requirements

- Claude Code installed and signed in
- A project folder
- Claude Code with custom subagent support
- PowerShell, Bash, or another shell supported by the project

Confirm Claude Code is available:

```powershell
claude --version
```

## Files

```text
.
├── README.md
└── Claude_Code_Reusable_Subagents.md
```

`Claude_Code_Reusable_Subagents.md` contains:

- The complete `project-builder` creation prompt
- The complete `project-verifier` creation prompt
- The complete `bug-fixer` creation prompt
- Agent validation instructions
- A reusable orchestration prompt for starting new projects

## Quick Start

Open PowerShell in the project you want to work on:

```powershell
cd "C:\Path\To\Your\Project"
claude
```

Open `Claude_Code_Reusable_Subagents.md` and paste each agent-creation prompt into Claude Code one at a time.

The definitions are created under:

```text
.claude/agents/
├── project-builder.md
├── project-verifier.md
└── bug-fixer.md
```

These are project-scoped agents. They are available in the current project without changing the agent setup for every other Claude Code project.

If `.claude/agents/` did not exist when the current Claude Code session started, exit and restart Claude Code after creating the first definition:

```text
/exit
```

```powershell
claude
```

## Validate the Agent Files

Ask Claude Code to validate and list the agents:

```text
Validate every file in .claude/agents and list the available project-scoped subagents with their names, purposes, models, turn limits, and allowed tools. Do not run them.
```

The directory can also be validated from PowerShell:

```powershell
claude plugin validate .claude/agents
```

## Start a Project

Copy the reusable orchestration prompt from `Claude_Code_Reusable_Subagents.md`. Replace these sections with the details of the project:

- `PROJECT GOAL`
- `PROJECT CONTEXT`
- `REQUIREMENTS`
- `ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA`
- `OUT OF SCOPE`

The main Claude Code session acts as the coordinator. It gives the implementation to `project-builder`, sends the finished work to `project-verifier`, and uses `bug-fixer` only when the verifier documents a confirmed problem.

## Review Reports

The verifier creates:

```text
verification_report.md
```

Each finding includes its severity, reproduction steps, expected behavior, actual behavior, evidence, and a recommended correction. The report ends with one of these verdicts:

- `PASS`
- `PASS WITH WARNINGS`
- `FAIL`

When repairs are needed, the bug fixer creates:

```text
bug_fix_report.md
```

The final review goes back to `project-verifier`, keeping verification separate from implementation and repair.

## Customize the Agents

The definitions can be adjusted per project. Common changes include:

- Increasing or reducing `maxTurns`
- Changing `model: inherit` to a supported model alias
- Removing tools an agent does not need
- Adding project-specific safety rules
- Adding required test, lint, build, or formatting commands
- Adding the project's definition of done

Keep the verifier read-only if you want an independent review. The default verifier can write its report and temporary test data, but it does not have the `Edit` tool.

## Safety Defaults

The prompts instruct the agents to:

- Avoid exposing credentials and private data
- Preserve unrelated work
- Use isolated or fabricated test data when appropriate
- Avoid deployments and external actions unless explicitly requested
- Require evidence before fixing a reported defect
- Return final verification to an independent agent

Review agent output before allowing actions involving production systems, real customer data, deployments, deletion, or external accounts.

## Current Status

This started as a test workflow while I was learning Claude Code subagents. I plan to keep improving the prompts as I use them across more projects and learn where the process can be tightened up.
